From: Shirogane Tsumugi <artoriapendragon123@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] spi: sun6i: support dual mode RX (DOUT reads)

The suniv SPI controller can clock reads out over both data pins, which
the driver never exposed. F1C200s manual 7.3.7.11 describes SPI_BCC_REG
(0x38) bit 28 as DRM, "Master Dual Mode RX Enable". The driver already
maps that register, it just uses the STC field of it.

Only RX is covered. There is no dual TX bit, and bit 29 (QUAD_EN on
A31/H3) is reserved on suniv anyway -- the pin list in manual 7.3.4 has
SPI_SCLK/SPI_MOSI/SPI_MISO/SPI_SS[3:0] and no D2/D3 -- so dual reads are
the only widening this SoC can do, and they need no extra wiring.

spi-mem splits a 1-1-2 op into single-bit TX transfers for cmd/addr/dummy
plus one RX-only transfer carrying rx_nbits = 2, so the STC/DBC burst
composition in the BCC register is never needed: the RX-only transfer
programs MBC/MTC/STC exactly as the single-bit path already does and only
adds DRM. This matches what the BSP driver does for DUAL_HALF_DUPLEX_RX
(spi_set_bc_tc_stc(0, len, 0, 0) plus spi_enable_dual()).

With this, spinand_select_op_variant() stops falling through to 0x0B Fast
Read and picks SPINAND_PAGE_READ_FROM_CACHE_X2_OP (0x3B) for chips whose
read_cache_variants offer it, halving the cache-to-host phase. The DMA
path is unaffected: bus width does not change how bytes land in the FIFO.

Requires spi-rx-bus-width = <2> on the slave node to take effect.

Signed-off-by: Shirogane Tsumugi <artoriapendragon123@gmail.com>

--- a/drivers/spi/spi-sun6i.c
+++ b/drivers/spi/spi-sun6i.c
@@ -83,6 +83,12 @@

 #define SUN6I_BURST_CTL_CNT_REG		0x38
 #define SUN6I_BURST_CTL_CNT_STC(cnt)		((cnt) & SUN6I_MAX_XFER_SIZE)
+/*
+ * DRM only switches the RX direction to two lines; there is no dual TX.
+ * On suniv bit 29 (QUAD_EN on A31/H3) is reserved and SPI_MOSI/SPI_MISO
+ * are the only data pins, so quad is not reachable here.
+ */
+#define SUN6I_BURST_CTL_CNT_DRM			BIT(28)

 #define SUN6I_TXDATA_REG		0x200
 #define SUN6I_RXDATA_REG		0x300
@@ -424,8 +430,16 @@
 	/* Setup the counters */
 	sun6i_spi_write(sspi, SUN6I_BURST_CNT_REG, SUN6I_BURST_CNT(tfr->len));
 	sun6i_spi_write(sspi, SUN6I_XMIT_CNT_REG, SUN6I_XMIT_CNT(tx_len));
-	sun6i_spi_write(sspi, SUN6I_BURST_CTL_CNT_REG,
-			SUN6I_BURST_CTL_CNT_STC(tx_len));
+
+	/*
+	 * The counters are unaffected by the bus width: a burst is always one
+	 * byte, dual mode just clocks it out in half the cycles. Plain
+	 * assignment also clears DRM for every non-dual transfer.
+	 */
+	reg = SUN6I_BURST_CTL_CNT_STC(tx_len);
+	if (rx_len && tfr->rx_nbits == SPI_NBITS_DUAL)
+		reg |= SUN6I_BURST_CTL_CNT_DRM;
+	sun6i_spi_write(sspi, SUN6I_BURST_CTL_CNT_REG, reg);

 	if (!use_dma) {
 		/* Fill the TX FIFO */
@@ -640,7 +654,8 @@
 	master->set_cs = sun6i_spi_set_cs;
 	master->transfer_one = sun6i_spi_transfer_one;
 	master->num_chipselect = 4;
-	master->mode_bits = SPI_CPOL | SPI_CPHA | SPI_CS_HIGH | SPI_LSB_FIRST;
+	master->mode_bits = SPI_CPOL | SPI_CPHA | SPI_CS_HIGH | SPI_LSB_FIRST |
+			    SPI_RX_DUAL;
 	master->bits_per_word_mask = SPI_BPW_MASK(8);
 	master->dev.of_node = pdev->dev.of_node;
 	master->auto_runtime_pm = true;
